A||||3||DaveL||2.5 Used Opposed to Rebuild||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||07-31-2001||03:28 PM||||I've heard how much cheaper it is to buy a used 2.5 out there, but when you add the cost of installation if you don't do it yerself, it still aint cheap. Also you end up with another tired engine that will potentially croak unexpectedly. My first 87 SE is sitting in my yard waiting for me to beg, borrow, steal, or save enough to rebuild or replace it. I'm not sure since I haven't torn it down yet, but I'm sure it threw a rod or something. One morning the usual knock got very loud, then after a muffled Kapow, and a cloud of white smoke out the exhaust, it died. Meanwhile my second 87 2.5 is as noisy as a diesel while at idle, making me paranoid that its on its way south too.<BR>What might it cost me to rebuild the engine in the first one? And should I go the whole route- crankshaft kit, bearings, camshaft, lifters, valves, pistons etc? Seems like I'd end up with a 2.5 that would last a good long time.....||||reg||5
Z||000001||862M4inCA||07-31-2001||06:38 PM||||Well as for the first engine (the one with a possible thrown rod) my biggest concern would be whether or not it punched a hole through the block. Secondly I would consider the cost of such a rebuild over purchacing a rebuilt motor from a parts store. About a year ago I had the 2.5 in mine go south while driving to LA. Burnt a hole right through the #3 piston. After consideration of parts and machine work to rebuild the motor I decided the $600 short block that Kragen wanted (parts store chain in CA for those who aren't familiar) wasn't such a bad buy. Granted I could have done the rebuild myself, but chances are that I would not have gotten a standard crank and cam (rather than having oversized) and would have had to possibly add those to the bill. Just my $02.||||reg||1
Z||000002||sprnt96||07-31-2001||06:57 PM||||I am in the same boat you are in so don't feel alone! I burnt a piston in my 84 SE I decided to go ahead and get it rebuilt instead of finding a used mtr, this way at least i have a 12k mile warranty. There is a long blk for sale with new parts that is going mighty cheap if someone is interested, I thought about it but i figured i might as well just get it done here at home where i know who is doing the machine work||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-31-2001||07:23 PM||||The DIS 4 was used from 87-91. they are cheap and plentiful at most junk yards in Olds Cutlass Calais and Grand Am. You need to put all the Fiero brackets/pulleys/wire on them.<P>Most of the DIS motors are noisy in the valve train. It appears to be a slight design defect but it generally doesn't hurt anything.<P>If you are knocking/pinging under certain load conditions try running Mobil 1 instead of regular oil and see if it helps. (It did for mine) GM got a little too agressive on the timing. There are PROM's to fix that problem but most people don't have a scanner. to read the ID code out of the ECM to get the right replacement chip. Mobil 1 quited mine enough I don't hear ping it but very rarely. Nice since I'd rather run that oil any way......||||reg||1
